 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
--   **Performance:** Using `SV_InstanceID` for data lookup is less efficient than using a second vertex buffer because the driver cannot inline the data, and hardware precaching/setup on the GPU wavefront is wasted as the hardware must look up the related array entry for each vertex [S1, unofficial].
--   **Driver Optimization:** The lack of inlining means that for each vertex, the hardware performs a memory lookup rather than loading the data once per instance [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Efficiency:** Using `SV_InstanceID` to look up data in a vertex shader is less efficient than using a second vertex buffer because the driver cannot inline the data, and hardware precaching/setup on the GPU wavefront is wasted as the hardware must look up the related array entry for each vertex [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Logic Requirement:** If no logic is implemented that depends on the `Instance ID`, all instances will have the same vertices [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
--   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot V)$, where $N$ is the number of instances and $V$ is the number of vertices per instance. However, the constant factor is higher due to memory lookup latency per vertex.
--   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing instance data in the buffer.
--   **Performance:** Less efficient than standard instancing with a second vertex buffer due to the inability of the driver to inline data and wasted hardware precaching [S1, unofficial].
--   **Strengths:** Flexible data access patterns; allows for complex, shader-driven instance state generation.
--   **Limitations:** Higher memory bandwidth usage per vertex; reduced driver optimization potential.
--   **When to Use:** When instance data is complex, dynamic, or requires shader-driven computation that cannot be easily pre-transformed into a vertex buffer.
--   **When Not to Use:** When performance is critical and instance data is simple (e.g., static transformations), in which case standard instancing with a second vertex buffer is preferred [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Lookup time is $O(1)$ per vertex, but involves a memory access to the instance buffer.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the instance data buffer.
+*   **Performance:** Less efficient than standard instancing with a second vertex buffer due to the inability to inline data and wasted hardware precaching [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Flexibility in accessing arbitrary instance data via indexing; can be used when a second vertex buffer is not available or practical.
+*   **Limitations:** Higher memory latency per vertex compared to standard instancing; potential for cache misses if instance data is not co-located with the accessed vertex data.
+*   **When to use:** When instance data is large, sparse, or requires complex indexing logic that doesn't fit well into a second vertex buffer layout.
+*   **When not to use:** When performance is critical and instance data is simple (e.g., just transformation matrices), as standard instancing with a second vertex buffer is more efficient [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This technique is a variation of hardware instancing, specifically utilizing the `SV_InstanceID` system value in Direct3D 11/12 and similar mechanisms in other APIs. It was discussed in the context of Direct3D 11 instancing on GameDev.StackExchange [S1, unofficial].
+This technique is part of the broader hardware instancing technology, which allows drawing many instances of geometry using a single GPU draw call [S1, unofficial]. The specific use of `SV_InstanceID` for lookup is a variation discussed in the context of Direct3D 11/12 programming [S1, unofficial].
 
